Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.200
Hello, and welcome back to another
video with ItsDrDan, and what we

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.410
are going to be learning is how
to find density via displacement.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.350
Now, the displacement method is
probably one of the most common

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:16.029
labs that's done in introductory
chemistry and general chemistry.

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:19.720
It's usually one of the very first
that you will have to do on your own.

00:00:20.134 --> 00:00:23.615
Trying to take different measurements
and observations and doing different

00:00:23.615 --> 00:00:27.885
calculations and it can be a little scary
the first time But it's just how can you

00:00:27.885 --> 00:00:32.414
use the different properties of matter
to your advantage thinking about how

00:00:32.435 --> 00:00:38.004
density is an intrinsic property Meaning
that it's unique for each material.

00:00:38.025 --> 00:00:42.405
It's generally very different from
one thing to the other And it's not

00:00:42.405 --> 00:00:48.665
affected by the amount of matter or mass
that you have So, if I have, let's say

00:00:48.665 --> 00:00:53.105
keep adding more and more of a, of a
substance, the density will not change.

00:00:53.105 --> 00:00:59.185
So you could have 10 grams of salt
or 10 million grams of salt, and they

00:00:59.185 --> 00:01:00.975
would still have the exact same density.

00:01:01.185 --> 00:01:06.144
It does not matter because it's
a ratio between mass over volume.

00:01:07.085 --> 00:01:07.775
So how

00:01:16.155 --> 00:01:19.045
exactly do we use due
to displacement method?

00:01:19.675 --> 00:01:21.815
Well, it's kind of a.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:26.120
It's, it's almost like following a
cookbook recipe when you're doing this.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:29.230
The first thing you're going to
do is measure the mass of your

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:34.050
object, um, because, and then also
be measuring the volume as well.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.829
Now, when we are doing this to
measure the volume, there are a

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:39.759
couple methods that you can use.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:44.110
You can just measure it maybe using
rulers and using known formulas.

00:01:44.755 --> 00:01:48.375
But like, for example, to solve the
volume of a cube or maybe the volume

00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:52.695
of a cylinder or whatever geometric
shape, but sometimes you might have

00:01:52.705 --> 00:01:57.035
weird shape things that don't quite
fit a certain conventional norm.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:00.124
So you have to sometimes use this method.

00:02:00.735 --> 00:02:03.235
So and it works for anything,
it could be even a normal

00:02:03.235 --> 00:02:04.295
shape, you could still use it.

00:02:04.865 --> 00:02:07.995
So what you're going to do, the first
thing is measure the mass of the object.

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:11.275
And then what you're going to
do is take a graduated cylinder.

00:02:11.605 --> 00:02:13.174
And fill it with a volume.

00:02:13.375 --> 00:02:16.305
Now, how much liquid should
you put in the first time?

00:02:17.035 --> 00:02:21.945
The idea about it is that you want
enough volume to submerge the object,

00:02:22.344 --> 00:02:26.884
but you don't want too much so that
way it overflows the graduated cylinder

00:02:27.115 --> 00:02:28.975
and you can't get a final measurement.

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:30.954
Because you're going to be
measuring the volume twice.

00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:33.165
It's about the difference of volume.

00:02:33.995 --> 00:02:37.975
So, going back to thinking about this,
so if I have density, we're going to

00:02:37.984 --> 00:02:40.774
have our grams or our mass on top.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:45.604
Now we're going to first measure the
volume, and then what we're then going

00:02:45.604 --> 00:02:50.995
to do is then put the object in, drop
it in there, and get a final volume,

00:02:51.065 --> 00:02:52.544
and we're going to measure both.

00:02:53.075 --> 00:02:56.415
So it's going to be final
minus initial volume.

00:02:56.905 --> 00:03:03.464
And that is how we're going to get our
density for this particular problem.

00:03:03.465 --> 00:03:05.495
This is going to be our formula
that we're going to use.

00:03:06.145 --> 00:03:09.885
So, just to kind of reiterate how
exactly does that look, let's take a

00:03:09.885 --> 00:03:13.645
look at a question to try to understand
this, and then we'll, I'll even

00:03:13.655 --> 00:03:15.465
illustrate what exactly this looks like.

00:03:16.325 --> 00:03:20.415
So, let's say if I have a lump of solid
material, meaning a lump is a really weird

00:03:20.415 --> 00:03:24.584
shape, um, and this was found to weigh 76.

00:03:24.585 --> 00:03:25.244
2 grams.

00:03:25.695 --> 00:03:31.315
So what I did is, I put my lump on the
balance and I saw, okay, this is 76.

00:03:31.315 --> 00:03:31.974
2, great.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.870
Now, what I'm going to do is, as you can
see, I have two graduated cylinders here.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.870
I first measured out 50 milliliters,
and I looked at my meniscus to

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.710
see that it was exactly on 50.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:49.810
Now, I'm gonna, I'm gonna slowly drop
my lump into the grad cylinder, and

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:55.580
when it sank to the bottom, I saw
that all of the liquid was displaced.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:56.650
It was moved.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:57.300
Why?

00:03:57.679 --> 00:04:03.290
Well, that is because this object
has so much volume to it that it's

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.149
going to displace all of the liquid.

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:10.990
When that happened, we
originally had 50 mls.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.860
It displaced it to 78.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.310
2, so let's see if we
can find the density.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:21.000
Let's go through this type of problem.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:25.109
So the first thing you want to do
is identify what all the important

00:04:25.109 --> 00:04:26.229
pieces of information are.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:32.200
The first thing you saw was, okay,
we had a mass, we had an initial

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:38.780
volume of water, and then we had a
final volume of water in the problem.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:43.140
So what we're going to do now is
write all those down for ourselves

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.080
so we can solve our problem.

00:04:45.899 --> 00:04:50.075
So, mass, was 76.

00:04:50.275 --> 00:04:54.945
2 grams, initial volume was 50.

00:04:55.055 --> 00:05:00.305
0 milliliters, and then
the final volume was 78.

00:05:00.365 --> 00:05:03.134
2 milliliters.

00:05:05.555 --> 00:05:11.325
Okay, now, when, how are we going, we're
going to put this into our formula, so

00:05:11.325 --> 00:05:15.224
density is equal to mass, so that's 76.

00:05:15.245 --> 00:05:21.625
2 grams, all over the volume, which
we have to do final minus initial.

00:05:21.725 --> 00:05:25.985
Don't forget that, that's probably one of
the most common mistakes students make is

00:05:25.985 --> 00:05:29.335
they forget to subtract your two volumes.

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:36.615
When we do that, we will get a, we
will simplify this, so it'd be 76.

00:05:36.655 --> 00:05:37.825
2 grams.

00:05:38.415 --> 00:05:39.870
and that will be divided by 28.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:42.365
2 milliliters.

00:05:42.925 --> 00:05:48.585
We will put that into our
calculators, and we will get 2.

00:05:48.585 --> 00:05:52.804
70 grams per milliliter,
or you could say 2.

00:05:52.804 --> 00:05:59.715
70 grams per centimeter cubed,
depending on which unit you prefer.

00:06:00.284 --> 00:06:02.395
Both are equivalent to each other.

00:06:02.755 --> 00:06:06.055
Don't forget one gram, or
sorry, one centimeter cubed.

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:08.980
is equal to one ml.

00:06:09.189 --> 00:06:13.179
That's a really important conversion to
kind of keep in the back of your mind.

00:06:13.179 --> 00:06:17.749
So one cc is a milliliter for
all my health science people.

00:06:17.749 --> 00:06:19.449
They always hear ccs, right?

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:22.250
That's a milliliter 2.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:22.735
7.

00:06:22.735 --> 00:06:24.140
So what does that mean?

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:25.759
What can we do with that
piece of information?

00:06:26.175 --> 00:06:28.825
Well, it allows you to try
to identify what it is.

00:06:28.865 --> 00:06:31.015
Remember, density is
an intrinsic property.

00:06:31.305 --> 00:06:34.115
It's unique for most substances, right?

00:06:34.135 --> 00:06:35.594
That's generally a different value.

00:06:35.615 --> 00:06:40.695
You can use it to try to eliminate
or figure out what could it be.

00:06:41.125 --> 00:06:43.835
So here I put a list
together of common metals.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.510
That you usually find in most
labs that are easily accessible.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.750
So we want one that's around 2.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:51.040
7 grams per milliliter.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:55.240
And what you will see is aluminum is 2.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.280
7 grams per milliliter.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:02.010
So that means that our lump
of material is aluminum.

00:07:02.369 --> 00:07:04.580
And that would be our final answer.

00:07:05.115 --> 00:07:07.495
And that's how you can use the
displacement method to help you.

00:07:08.915 --> 00:07:11.115
It's a little short video
just to give you the ideas.

